  "body": "## What was wrong\n\nAuto-refresh re-ran the two most expensive endpoints every 15 seconds regardless of whether anything had happened. On an idle dashboard that is percentiles, three `GROUP BY` breakdowns and a series bucket — forever, for no new data.\n\n## The change\n\n**A heartbeat endpoint.** `GET /admin/api/requests/pulse` answers one `COUNT`/`MAX` under the same filters. Auto-refresh polls that and only falls through to the full reload when the row count or newest timestamp actually moved. **11 ms against ~800 ms** for `stats()` on a 50k-row log.\n\nThe tab also stops polling entirely while hidden (and catches up on becoming visible), and the interval is selectable — 5/15/30/60s instead of fixed 15.\n\n**The baseline is established by the first pulse, not by the preceding full load.** The list query is paged, so its newest visible row is not `MAX(ts_epoch)` once you are past page one; seeding from it would make every later tick look changed and reload the whole view — exactly the cost this removes. It is also keyed by the filter signature, since a different window has different counts and comparing across a filter change reports movement that only happened because the question changed.\n\n**Three server-side fixes in `stats()`:**\n\n- Percentiles no longer pull every `duration_ms` into Python to sort — the cursor is streamed and abandoned once the highest needed rank has gone past.\n- The stats cache was an **unbounded dict** keyed by the full filter tuple, leaking a full payload per distinct filter combination for the life of the process. Now a 64-entry LRU that also drops entries on expiry.\n- Each breakdown is capped at 50 rows by volume with a `*_truncated` flag **surfaced in the UI**. A gateway with hundreds of models returned hundreds of rows on every poll. The cap is never silent.\n\n## What did not work, recorded so it is not repeated\n\nAn index leading on `duration_ms` makes the percentile lookup **68x faster in isolation** and made the whole of `stats()` **2.2x slower**:\n\n```\nwith    idx_requests_duration_v1:  stats() 1525 ms\nwithout idx_requests_duration_v1:  stats()  701 ms\n```\n\nSame database, same process, differing only by the index existing. With no `ANALYZE` statistics SQLite starts preferring it for the totals and breakdown aggregates, which it does not cover — turning one clean scan of `idx_requests_stats_v3` into an index seek plus a table lookup per row. It would also have meant building an index over a 1.2 GB `requests.db` on first start. **Not added.**\n\n## Verification\n\nPercentiles match the removed helper **exactly** on the same data, filtered and unfiltered:\n\n```\nunfiltered p50 498.98 == 498.98      filtered p50 498.22 == 498.22\nunfiltered p95 949.88 == 949.88      filtered p95 949.64 == 949.64\nempty window -> None\n```\n\nAlternating A/B against `main`, median of 7 per round, same synthetic 50k-row DB — alternated because a first attempt measured while test suites were running was not trustworthy:\n\n```\nround 1   main 1182 ms   this branch 1157 ms\nround 2   main  935 ms   this branch  706 ms\nround 3   main 1107 ms   this branch  873 ms\n```\n\nLevel to ~20% faster. **The honest headline is the pulse endpoint, not `stats()`** — the win is that the expensive query stops running at all when nothing changed.\n\nLive server on a throwaway `HOME`:\n\n```\n/admin/api/requests/pulse                     {\"total\":0,\"last_ts\":null,\"enabled\":true}  [200 0.0157s]\n/admin/api/requests/pulse?provider=nvidia_nim {\"total\":0,\"last_ts\":null,\"enabled\":true}  [200]\n```\n\n```\n$ uv run ty check\nAll checks passed!\n\n$ env -u ENABLE_WEB_SERVER_TOOLS -u EXA_API_KEY uv run pytest -q\n6 failed, 3273 passed, 25 skipped in 236.36s\n```\n\nThe 6 are the PowerShell installer tests, which never run on Linux CI. Rebased onto `3c5955b` (4.32.0) and re-verified there, since both intervening releases changed `admin.js`.\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- **The visibility pause, the interval selector and the truncation note have not been driven in a browser.** Logic verified by reading and by the live endpoint responses above.\n- The breakdown cap slightly reduces filter autocomplete suggestions, which are built from those same breakdowns. Visible via the truncation note.\n- Benchmarks are on a synthetic 50k-row database, not the real 1.2 GB log. Absolute numbers there will be larger; the relative comparison is what these support.\n- `pulse()` cannot detect a change where one row is pruned and another added between ticks such that both count and max timestamp land identically. Vanishingly unlikely, and the next real request corrects it.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
