    "message": "perf(analytics): poll a cheap heartbeat instead of the whole dashboard\n\nAuto-refresh re-ran the two most expensive endpoints every 15 seconds\nregardless of whether anything had happened. On an idle dashboard that is\npercentiles, three GROUP BY breakdowns and a series bucket, forever, for no\nnew data.\n\nNew GET /admin/api/requests/pulse answers one COUNT/MAX under the same\nfilters. Auto-refresh polls that and only falls through to the full reload\nwhen the row count or newest timestamp actually moved. Measured on a 50k-row\nlog: 11 ms against ~800 ms for stats(). The tab also stops polling entirely\nwhile hidden and catches up on becoming visible again, and the interval is\nselectable (5/15/30/60s) instead of fixed.\n\nThe baseline the pulse compares against is established by the first pulse,\nnot by the preceding full load. The list query is paged, so its newest\nvisible row is not MAX(ts_epoch) once you are past page one; seeding from it\nwould make every later tick look changed and reload the whole view -- exactly\nthe cost this is meant to remove. It is also keyed by the filter signature,\nbecause a different window has different counts and comparing across a filter\nchange reports movement that only happened because the question changed.\n\nServer-side, three things in stats():\n\n- Percentiles no longer pull every duration_ms into Python to sort. The\n  cursor is streamed and abandoned once the highest needed rank has gone past.\n- The stats cache was an unbounded dict keyed by the full filter tuple, so\n  every distinct filter combination leaked a full payload for the life of the\n  process. It is now a 64-entry LRU that also drops entries on expiry.\n- Each breakdown is capped at 50 rows ordered by volume, with a *_truncated\n  flag surfaced in the UI. A gateway with hundreds of models returned hundreds\n  of rows on every poll. The cap is never silent.\n\nA note on what did NOT work, because it is worth not repeating: an index\nleading on duration_ms makes the percentile lookup 68x faster in isolation and\nmade the whole of stats() 2.2x SLOWER -- 1525 ms against 701 ms on the same\ndatabase, differing only by the index existing. With no ANALYZE statistics\nSQLite starts preferring it for the totals and breakdown aggregates, which it\ndoes not cover, turning one clean scan of idx_requests_stats_v3 into an index\nseek plus a table lookup per row. It would also have meant building an index\nover a 1.2 GB requests.db on first start. Not added.\n\nVerification. Percentiles match the removed helper exactly on the same data,\nfiltered and unfiltered:\n\n    unfiltered p50 498.98 == 498.98    filtered p50 498.22 == 498.22\n    unfiltered p95 949.88 == 949.88    filtered p95 949.64 == 949.64\n    empty window -> None\n\nAlternating A/B against main, median of 7 per round, same synthetic 50k-row\ndatabase (alternated because a first attempt measured while test suites were\nrunning was not trustworthy):\n\n    round 1   main 1182 ms   this branch 1157 ms\n    round 2   main  935 ms   this branch  706 ms\n    round 3   main 1107 ms   this branch  873 ms\n\nLevel to ~20% faster; the honest headline is the pulse endpoint, not stats().\n\nLive server on a throwaway HOME: pulse returns {\"total\":0,\"last_ts\":null,\n\"enabled\":true} in 15 ms and honours provider filters. Full suite 6 failed,\n3273 passed -- the 6 are the PowerShell installer tests that never run on\nLinux CI. ruff, ty and node --check clean.\n\nNot verified: the visibility pause, the interval selector and the truncation\nnote have not been driven in a browser.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
